Critical clients need their own capacity. A single shared limit can still let one failed or noisy workload consume every available slot.

> Protect critical clients by giving them their own pool to call.

Source: [Q07](/lore/durability-bibliography#q07); [B4](/lore/durability-bibliography#b4-release-it), [B3](/lore/durability-bibliography#b3-designing-elixir-systems-with-otp).

## Failure it prevents

Noncritical load exhausts the pool needed by the primary path.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

Separate `Semaphore` or `Queue` instances isolate local work admission. `Pool` manages actual resources. Independent runtime or infrastructure failure domains require more than fibers.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Semaphore.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Queue.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Pool.ts`.

## How to check

Saturate one workload. Confirm that the separately reserved critical path still acquires its resources. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Backpressure reaches the producer](/lore/backpressure-reaches-the-producer), [Effect is not BEAM](/lore/effect-is-not-beam).

Connect this to [the existing rat-stack guidance](/lore/scopes-own-resources).

